{
  "concept": "A conic whose axes are tilted relative to the x,y axes picks up an xy cross term; rotating the coordinate frame by the tilt angle θ into x',y' aligns the axes with the conic and eliminates that term.",
  "figureType": "diagram",
  "elements": [
    "Thin black double-headed horizontal axis with arrowheads at both ends, labeled 'x' at its right end",
    "Thin black double-headed vertical axis with arrowheads at both ends, labeled 'y' at its top",
    "Origin where both frames intersect (common center)",
    "Orange double-headed axis running lower-left to upper-right, labeled x' in orange italic near its upper-right arrowhead",
    "Orange double-headed axis running lower-right to upper-left (perpendicular to x'), labeled y' in orange italic near its upper-left arrowhead",
    "Dark navy-blue ellipse outline centered at the origin, major axis along x', minor axis along y', unfilled",
    "Small black curved arc between the positive x-axis and the positive x'-axis, labeled 'θ' in italic",
    "Equation readout panel showing the conic in the currently selected frame (general form with Bxy term, or aligned standard form in x',y')",
    "Highlight emphasis on the xy term / on the x'²,y'² terms depending on the selected frame",
    "Clickable marker point on the ellipse (small filled dot)",
    "Dashed black projection segments from the marker point to the x and y axes with foot-point ticks",
    "Dashed orange projection segments from the marker point to the x' and y' axes with foot-point ticks",
    "Two coordinate readout callouts near the marker: (x, y) in black and (x', y') in orange",
    "Legend/key distinguishing the fixed black frame from the rotated orange frame",
    "No tick marks or numeric labels on either axis set"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "thetaSlider",
      "type": "slider",
      "teaches": "That θ is the single angle measuring how far the conic's own axes are turned from the x,y axes; sweeping it turns the orange frame and the ellipse together, and only at the true tilt angle does the frame align with the ellipse.",
      "label": "Rotation angle θ",
      "range": [0, 90, 1],
      "default": 40
    },
    {
      "id": "frameToggle",
      "type": "toggle",
      "teaches": "That the same ellipse has two algebraic descriptions: a messy Ax²+Bxy+Cy²=1 in the fixed frame and a clean x'²/a²+y'²/b²=1 in the rotated frame — the xy term is the algebraic signature of the tilt.",
      "label": "Equation frame",
      "options": ["Original (x, y)", "Rotated (x', y')"],
      "default": "Original (x, y)"
    },
    {
      "id": "pointProbe",
      "type": "click",
      "teaches": "That a single physical point on the ellipse carries two different coordinate pairs, related by x'=x cosθ+y sinθ and y'=-x sinθ+y cosθ — the point never moves, only the ruler does."
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "Aligned conic: no tilt",
      "narration": "With θ = 0 the orange frame sits exactly on top of the black frame and the ellipse's major axis lies along x. The equation has no xy term.",
      "state": { "thetaSlider": 0, "frameToggle": "Original (x, y)" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Turn the conic away from the axes",
      "narration": "Drag θ upward: the ellipse and the orange axes rotate together while x and y stay fixed. As soon as θ ≠ 0 an xy term appears in the (x, y) equation and grows with the tilt.",
      "state": { "thetaSlider": 40, "frameToggle": "Original (x, y)" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Read the same conic in the rotated frame",
      "narration": "Switch the equation panel to the primed frame. The identical curve is now written as x'²/a² + y'²/b² = 1 — the cross term is gone because the axes match the ellipse.",
      "state": { "thetaSlider": 40, "frameToggle": "Rotated (x', y')" }
    },
    {
      "title": "One point, two coordinate pairs",
      "narration": "Click any point on the ellipse. Black dashed projections give (x, y); orange dashed projections give (x', y'). The point is unchanged — only the measuring frame differs.",
      "state": { "thetaSlider": 40, "frameToggle": "Rotated (x', y')" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Extreme tilt",
      "narration": "Push θ toward 90°: the ellipse's long axis swings onto the y-axis. The xy term shrinks back to zero at exactly 90°, showing the term measures misalignment, not shape.",
      "state": { "thetaSlider": 88, "frameToggle": "Original (x, y)" }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "The xy term in a conic's equation is not a property of the conic — it is a property of the coordinate frame; rotate the frame by θ and the term disappears while the curve itself never moves.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Square plane diagram, white background, origin centered. Fixed frame: thin black double-headed arrows spanning the full width and height, italic 'x' at the right end just below the axis, italic 'y' at the top. Rotated frame: two thicker orange (vermilion) double-headed arrows through the origin at angle θ and θ+90°, extending slightly beyond the black axes, labeled x' (upper right) and y' (upper left) in bold orange italic. Ellipse: dark navy outline, ~2 mm stroke, centered at origin, major-to-minor ratio roughly 2:1, major axis on x', large enough to cross the black horizontal axis on both sides and reach above and below the black vertical axis. A small black arc of radius about one-fifth the ellipse's semi-major axis sweeps from the positive x-axis to the positive x'-axis with an arrowhead, labeled italic θ just outside the arc. Equation panel in a corner, monospaced-ish math text; the xy coefficient term is highlighted orange when the original frame is shown, and the x'² / y'² terms highlighted orange when the rotated frame is shown. Clicked point: solid navy dot on the ellipse with black dashed drop-lines to x and y and orange dashed drop-lines to x' and y', plus two small readout labels (x, y) in black and (x', y') in orange. No tick marks or numbers anywhere on the axes."
}

